<ArticleLayout
  title="Deciphering the Thunderous Echoes of Rocketry: A Study in Acoustic Dynamics"
  description="A note on measured Space Launch System acoustics and their relevance to launch vehicles, facilities, payloads, and surrounding communities."
  date="December 18, 2023"
  category="Research note / launch acoustics"
  canonicalPath="/2023/12/18/deciphering-the-thunderous-echoes-of-rocketry-a-study-in-acoustic-dynamics/"
>
  <p>A study of Space Launch System acoustics, <em>Space Launch System acoustics: Far-field noise measurements of the Artemis-I launch</em> by Kent L. Gee and collaborators, reports post-liftoff noise levels up to 136 dB and an average maximum one-third-octave spectral peak at 20 Hz.</p>
  <p>These measurements matter because the launch acoustic environment influences launch-pad, vehicle, and payload design. Better characterization also supports the assessment of community and environmental impacts.</p>
  <p>For the technical details, see the <a href="https://doi.org/10.1121/10.0016878" rel="noreferrer">referenced study via DOI</a>.</p>
</ArticleLayout>
